---
export const prerender = true

import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCode from "../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";
import DocCard from "../../components/docs/DocCard.astro";

const coreShellShots = [
  {
    src: "/docs/tui/home.png",
    alt: "nikcli TUI home screen with empty prompt and placeholder text",
    label: "Home",
    caption: "Empty home with localized placeholder and selected agent.",
  },
  {
    src: "/docs/tui/slash-menu.png",
    alt: "Slash command autocomplete popover after typing /",
    label: "Slash autocomplete",
    caption:
      "Typing ‘/’ opens the slash-command autocomplete popover over the prompt.",
  },
  {
    src: "/docs/tui/command-palette.png",
    alt: "Command palette dialog with suggested and system commands",
    label: "Command palette",
    caption:
      "Ctrl+P opens the full command palette with Suggested and System categories.",
  },
];

const inAppDialogShots = [
  {
    src: "/docs/tui/context-usage.png",
    alt:
      "Context usage dialog with prompt composition, top sources, tokens-per-turn sparkline and context health",
    label: "Context usage",
    caption:
      "Live context window pressure, cache efficiency, prompt composition, top sources and context health with fragmentation flag.",
  },
  {
    src: "/docs/tui/analytics-overview.png",
    alt:
      "Analytics overview dialog with session, message, cost and token KPIs, last-30-days charts and operational health",
    label: "Analytics — Overview",
    caption:
      "Tabbed analytics dashboard: sessions, messages, cost, tokens KPIs; tokens/day histogram, cost sparkline, usage composition donut, efficiency gauges, operational health.",
  },
];

const workspaceShots = [
  {
    src: "/docs/tui/workspace-sessions.png",
    alt:
      "Workspace view with Sessions tab active, focus on a session tree showing 1 root and 674 sessions, with a branch expanded into two delegated tasks",
    label: "Workspace — Sessions",
    caption:
      "Four-tab workspace view: Sessions (session tree with focus and timeline), Changes (git diff and stage/unstage), Graph (commit graph), GitHub (PR / branch / workflow). Cycle with tab or slash commands /tree, /changes, /graph, /github.",
  },
];

const toc = [
  { title: "Overview", href: "#overview", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Visual tour", href: "#visual-tour", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Default command", href: "#default-command", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Worker model", href: "#worker-model", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Transport modes", href: "#transport-modes", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Renderer tree", href: "#renderer-tree", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Feature plugins", href: "#feature-plugins", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Dialogs", href: "#dialogs", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Routes", href: "#routes", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Commands and keybinds", href: "#commands-keybinds", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Event flow", href: "#event-flow", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Island plugin", href: "#island", depth: 2 },
  { title: "HTTP control routes", href: "#http-control", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Config and plugins", href: "#config-plugins", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Remote and attach", href: "#remote-attach", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Operational checks", href: "#operational-checks", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Failure modes", href: "#failure-modes", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Source anchors", href: "#source-anchors", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="TUI"
  subtitle="Terminal UI entrypoint, worker RPC, OpenTUI renderer, command registry, routes, event streams, and remote control wiring."
  description="Guide to nikcli’s terminal UI, worker RPC, OpenTUI renderer, command routes, event streams, and remote control."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The nikcli terminal UI is the default command registered by
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/thread.ts</code>. It is not only a renderer. The TUI is a
      split runtime: the main thread owns terminal input, OpenTUI rendering, dialogs, routes, and
      local process lifecycle, while a Bun worker owns the server app, project instances, SDK fetches,
      event streams, upgrades, and cleanup.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Default route" eyebrow="$0 [project]">
        <p>The bare <code>nikcli</code> command starts the TUI, resolves the working directory, validates any requested session, and passes initial args into the renderer.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Worker RPC" eyebrow="Bun Worker">
        <p>The worker exposes fetch, server startup, event subscription, reload, upgrade, and shutdown methods through <code>Rpc.client</code>.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="OpenTUI renderer" eyebrow="Solid">
        <p><code>app.tsx</code> builds the Solid provider tree, command registry, route switch, dialogs, plugin routes, and keyboard handling.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="SDK surface" eyebrow="@nikcli-ai/sdk/v2">
        <p>The UI talks to the same Hono app as external clients, either through in-process fetch or through an HTTP server when network access is enabled.</p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="visual-tour" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Visual tour</h2>
    <p>
      The captures on this page come from two sources: a recorded
      <code>bun run --cwd packages/nikcli dev</code> session run through a terminal-control
      PTY at 112×34 with the OpenTUI host profile, and live in-session dialogs opened
      during real workflows. Bundles, raw <code>.termctrl</code> recordings, accessibility
      trees, SVGs, PNGs and manifests live under <code>artifacts/tui/tui-check/</code>; the
      same skill powers the <a href="/docs/terminal-control">terminal-control</a> docs.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-shell">Core shell</h3>
    <p>
      Empty home, slash-command autocomplete, and command palette — the surfaces reached
      before submitting a prompt.
    </p>
    <div class="tui-gallery not-prose" data-count={coreShellShots.length}>
      {
        coreShellShots.map((shot) => (
          <figure class="tui-gallery__shot">
            <a
              class="tui-gallery__link"
              href={shot.src}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              aria-label={`Open ${shot.label} screenshot in a new tab`}
            >
              <img
                src={shot.src}
                alt={shot.alt}
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                width="2088"
                height="1296"
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption class="tui-gallery__caption">
              <span class="tui-gallery__label">{shot.label}</span>
              <span class="tui-gallery__hint">{shot.caption}</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <h3 id="in-app-surfaces">In-app surfaces</h3>
    <p>
      Full-screen dialogs that the TUI renders on top of the shell: context-usage telemetry
      and analytics with KPIs, charts, gauges, and operational health badges.
    </p>
    <div class="tui-gallery tui-gallery--two not-prose" data-count={inAppDialogShots.length}>
      {
        inAppDialogShots.map((shot) => (
          <figure class="tui-gallery__shot">
            <a
              class="tui-gallery__link"
              href={shot.src}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              aria-label={`Open ${shot.label} screenshot in a new tab`}
            >
              <img
                src={shot.src}
                alt={shot.alt}
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                width="2088"
                height="1296"
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption class="tui-gallery__caption">
              <span class="tui-gallery__label">{shot.label}</span>
              <span class="tui-gallery__hint">{shot.caption}</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <h3 id="workspace-view">Workspace view</h3>
    <p>
      The workspace route (<code>type: "workspace"</code>, defined in
      <code>context/route.tsx</code>) is a four-tab panel that overlays any session. Each tab is
      registered as a hidden command in <code>app.tsx</code> (lines 714–787) and reachable from the
      command palette or as a slash command.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Tab</th><th>Slash</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Sessions</strong></td><td><code>/tree</code></td><td>Session tree with focus and timeline: shows every session for the active workspace, marks the focused session, and supports the delegated-task hierarchy (root, subagents, plan/decision points).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Changes</strong></td><td><code>/changes</code></td><td>Git diff with stage/unstage, file and hunk navigation, commit, push, and PR publish.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Graph</strong></td><td><code>/graph</code> · <code>/gitgraph</code> · <code>/commits</code></td><td>Commit graph with log search, branch/lane navigation, and one-key checkout or reset.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>GitHub</strong></td><td><code>/github</code> · <code>/gh</code></td><td>GitHub integration: PR list, create/merge PR, run workflow, branch protection status.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="tui-gallery tui-gallery--two not-prose" data-count={workspaceShots.length}>
      {
        workspaceShots.map((shot) => (
          <figure class="tui-gallery__shot">
            <a
              class="tui-gallery__link"
              href={shot.src}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              aria-label={`Open ${shot.label} screenshot in a new tab`}
            >
              <img
                src={shot.src}
                alt={shot.alt}
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                width="2088"
                height="1296"
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption class="tui-gallery__caption">
              <span class="tui-gallery__label">{shot.label}</span>
              <span class="tui-gallery__hint">{shot.caption}</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <DocCallout type="info" title="Reproducible captures">
      <p>
        Re-run the recorded shell capture with
        <code>bun run --cwd packages/terminal-control control -- start tui-check
        --host opentui --cols 112 --rows 34 --record /tmp/tui-check.termctrl
        -- env NIKCLI_TERMINAL=1 bun run --cwd /Volumes/.../packages/nikcli dev</code>,
        then re-bundle per marker:
        <code>--at-marker home|slash-menu|palette --result passed ...</code>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="default-command" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Default command</h2>
    <p>
      <code>TuiThreadCommand</code> is registered in <code>packages/nikcli/src/index.ts</code> before
      the one-shot run command. The command name is <code>$0 [project]</code>, which makes it the
      default yargs route. It accepts project path, model, continue, session, prompt, and agent
      options, plus shared network flags from <code>withNetworkOptions</code>.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Runtime effect</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>[project]</code></td><td>Resolved by <code>resolveThreadDirectory</code>; relative paths are based on <code>PWD</code> so shell <code>--cwd</code> behavior stays intact.</td><td><code>thread.ts</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--model</code></td><td>Forwarded into the TUI args context as the initial provider/model selection.</td><td><code>thread.ts</code>, <code>context/args</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--continue</code></td><td>Signals that the UI should continue the latest session instead of starting from an empty home route.</td><td><code>thread.ts</code>, session route code</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--session</code></td><td>Validated with <code>Session.ID.safeParse</code> and <code>sdk.session.get</code> before the renderer starts.</td><td><code>validateSession</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--prompt</code></td><td>Combined with piped stdin when stdin is not interactive, then passed into <code>ArgsProvider</code>.</td><td><code>thread.ts</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--agent</code></td><td>Forwarded into the args context so session startup can select the requested agent.</td><td><code>thread.ts</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--port</code>, <code>--hostname</code>, <code>--mdns</code></td><td>Switch the TUI from in-process RPC transport to a worker-hosted HTTP server.</td><td><code>resolveNetworkOptions</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="PTY detection matters">
      <p>
        Mobile and nikcli-managed PTYs set <code>NIKCLI_TERMINAL=1</code>. The TUI treats that as an
        interactive terminal even if Bun reports <code>stdin.isTTY</code> as false, avoiding the
        piped-stdin path that would otherwise block renderer startup.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="worker-model" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Worker model</h2>
    <p>
      The main process chooses <code>NIKCLI_WORKER_PATH</code>, the built worker file, or the local
      TypeScript worker source. It then starts a Bun <code>Worker</code> with a sanitized environment
      where <code>Process.ROLE_ENV</code> is <code>worker</code> and a run id is guaranteed. The worker
      calls <code>Process.ensureMetadata("worker")</code>, initializes logging, listens for global
      bus events, and exposes an RPC object.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>RPC method</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Operational detail</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>fetch</code></td><td>Runs SDK requests against <code>Server.App().fetch</code>.</td><td>Adds Basic Auth from server flags when configured and returns status, headers, and text body.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>server</code></td><td>Starts or restarts a Bun Hono server from the worker.</td><td>Stops the old server before replacing it; returns the concrete URL.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>subscribe</code></td><td>Starts an event stream for a project directory.</td><td>Creates an SDK client against <code>http://nikcli.local</code> using worker-local fetch and emits each event back through RPC.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>unsubscribe</code></td><td>Stops a worker event stream.</td><td>Aborts the matching stream controller and removes it from the stream map.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>checkUpgrade</code></td><td>Runs passive upgrade checks after the renderer starts.</td><td>Executes inside <code>Instance.provide</code> for the current directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>upgradeNow</code></td><td>Runs an explicit upgrade requested by the TUI.</td><td>Delegates to <code>upgradeNow(method, version)</code> inside the project instance.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>reload</code></td><td>Disposes all project instances after <code>SIGUSR2</code>.</td><td>Used by development reload flows.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>shutdown</code></td><td>Stops event streams, disposes instances, and stops the HTTP server.</td><td>The main thread waits up to five seconds before terminating the worker.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Shutdown is explicit">
      <p>
        The TUI does not rely on worker process exit for cleanup. <code>onExit</code> calls worker
        shutdown, <code>onBeforeExit</code> disposes TUI plugin runtime state, and the main thread
        terminates the worker only after the RPC shutdown path returns or times out.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="transport-modes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Transport modes</h2>
    <p>
      The TUI has two runtime transports. Both use the generated SDK surface, but they differ in how
      requests and events reach the server app.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>When used</th><th>Fetch path</th><th>Event path</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Direct worker transport</td><td>No explicit port, hostname, mdns, or non-local configured server values.</td><td><code>createWorkerFetch</code> forwards the request over RPC to <code>worker.fetch</code>.</td><td><code>createEventSource</code> subscribes through worker RPC and receives <code>event</code> emissions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Worker HTTP server</td><td>Network options request an externally reachable server.</td><td>The SDK uses normal <code>fetch</code> against the returned HTTP URL.</td><td><code>SDKProvider</code> opens server SSE through <code>sdk.event.subscribe</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Server on demand">
      <p>
        Even when the TUI starts in direct mode, the session UI can later call the
        <code>startServer</code> callback. That asks the worker to bind a local server on port
        <code>0</code> and returns the URL for local share or preview flows.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="renderer-tree" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Renderer tree</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/app.tsx</code> creates an OpenTUI renderer with a 45 FPS
      target, Kitty keyboard support, mouse support from TUI config, copy selection handling, and
      explicit Ctrl-C behavior. The Solid app is mounted inside an error boundary and a deep provider
      stack.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Provider group</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>ArgsProvider</code>, <code>ExitProvider</code>, <code>ServerProvider</code></td><td>Expose command args, restart/exit hooks, server startup, and plugin disposal on exit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>SDKProvider</code>, <code>ProjectProvider</code>, <code>SyncProvider</code></td><td>Create the SDK client, subscribe to project/server events, and keep synchronized runtime state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>AnalyticsProvider</code>, <code>KVProvider</code>, <code>LocalProvider</code></td><td>Expose usage, local preferences, model favorites, and small local state to routes and dialogs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ThemeProvider</code>, <code>KeybindProvider</code>, <code>AttentionProvider</code></td><td>Apply terminal palette mode, user keybinds, and attention/notification handling.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>DialogProvider</code>, <code>CommandProvider</code>, <code>ToastProvider</code></td><td>Manage dialogs, slash commands, keyboard command triggers, and toast rendering.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>PromptHistoryProvider</code>, <code>PromptStashProvider</code>, <code>PromptRefProvider</code></td><td>Power prompt history, frecency, stash, and imperative prompt operations.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>EditorContextProvider</code>, <code>UpgradeProvider</code></td><td>Expose editor integration state and interactive upgrade actions.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="feature-plugins" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Feature plugins</h2>
    <p>
      Almost every surface the TUI shows — home tips, sidebar panels, the plugin manager,
      the wallpaper, the loop and mission managers — is a self-contained feature plugin
      under <code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/feature-plugins/</code>, loaded through the
      same runtime that loads third-party plugins from npm. The larger ones follow the same
      layout: <code>store.ts</code> for reactive state, <code>sdk.ts</code> for typed SDK
      bindings, <code>runner.ts</code> for the local control plane, and <code>dialogs.tsx</code>
      for the Solid components.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>home</code></td><td>Rotating tip line in the <code>home.bottom</code> slot, plus the <code>tips.toggle</code> command.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sidebar</code></td><td>Six independent slot plugins: context/cost, MCP, LSP, todo, modified files, and the footer.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>system</code></td><td>The plugin manager (list, toggle, install) and the OpenRouter Fusion preset manager.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>background</code></td><td>Paints a real image behind the TUI via OpenTUI's native half-block super-sampler; owns <code>/background</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loops</code></td><td><a href="/docs/loops">Loops</a> manager, creation wizard and live sidebar. Mirrors the loop SDK contract.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mission</code></td><td><a href="/docs/missions">Missions</a> manager, planning wizard and live sidebar. Mirrors the mission SDK contract.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>brain</code></td><td>Arms the Brain consolidation scheduler and registers <code>/brain</code> for an on-demand pass.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>browser</code></td><td>Browser-use control surface for the <code>browser</code> tool (page navigation, snapshots, action log).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>chatbot</code></td><td>Chat SDK bots — start/stop per platform and show the webhook path each one must be pointed at.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>computer</code></td><td>Probes the host and reports what the <code>computer</code> tool can do on this machine.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>connectors</code></td><td>Live connector health, cache invalidation, and stored-credential removal inside the TUI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>observability</code></td><td>Live OpenTelemetry spans and OTLP endpoint dialogs (see <a href="/docs/observability">Observability</a>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>island</code></td><td>Toggles the macOS Island notch integration for the current TUI session. Registered only under <code>--island</code>. See <a href="#island">Island plugin</a> below.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Each plugin's commands, slash aliases, KV keys and internals are documented in
      <a href="/docs/tui-plugins">TUI feature plugins</a>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="TUI plugin runtime">
      <p>
        Plugins register commands, routes, dialogs, keybinds, and sidebar / status slots through
        <code>cli/cmd/tui/plugin/api.tsx</code>. Runtime cleanup runs on exit through
        <code>TuiPluginRuntime.dispose()</code> — plugins should not rely on process exit for
        teardown.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="dialogs" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Dialogs</h2>
    <p>
      All dialogs live in <code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/component/</code> and are
      registered through the <code>DialogProvider</code> and the command palette. The full list
      (in addition to those in the top-level README):
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Dialog</th><th>Source</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-telemetry-live</code></td><td><code>dialog-telemetry-live.tsx</code></td><td>Scrolling, filterable list of live OpenTelemetry spans (see <a href="/docs/observability">Observability</a>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-opentelemetry</code></td><td><code>dialog-opentelemetry.tsx</code></td><td>OTLP configuration dialog: shows the resolved endpoint, the resource block, and a "Copy as env" action.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-advisor-model</code></td><td><code>dialog-advisor-model.tsx</code></td><td>Pick the model used by the <code>advisor</code> tool.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-browser-use</code></td><td><code>dialog-browser-use.tsx</code></td><td>Browser-use control surface: live snapshot, navigation, action log for the <code>browser</code> tool.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-routine</code></td><td><code>dialog-routine.tsx</code></td><td>Routine create/edit dialog backed by the routine SDK.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-session-warp</code></td><td><code>dialog-session-warp.tsx</code></td><td>Quick-jump to another session by fuzzy match.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-session-rename</code></td><td><code>dialog-session-rename.tsx</code></td><td>Rename the active session.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-session-delete-failed</code></td><td><code>dialog-session-delete-failed.tsx</code></td><td>Confirmation when a session delete failed and the local cache is stale.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-mobile-connect</code></td><td><code>dialog-mobile-connect.tsx</code></td><td>Show a pairing QR code and bearer token, plus copy-to-clipboard, from inside the TUI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-sync</code></td><td><code>dialog-sync.tsx</code></td><td><a href="/docs/sync">Sync</a> status, hub URL/token config, autostart toggle, and reconnect.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-teleport</code></td><td><code>dialog-teleport.tsx</code></td><td>Pick a session and remote target, then ship the session through <code>nikcli teleport</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-workspace-create</code></td><td><code>dialog-workspace-create.tsx</code></td><td>Create a new multi-project workspace.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-workspace-list</code></td><td><code>dialog-workspace-list.tsx</code></td><td>Switch between workspaces.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-workspace-unavailable</code></td><td><code>dialog-workspace-unavailable.tsx</code></td><td>Show the error that prevents entering a workspace.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dialog-workspace-file-changes</code></td><td><code>dialog-workspace-file-changes.tsx</code></td><td>Aggregate file changes across the workspace's projects.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plugin-route-missing</code></td><td><code>plugin-route-missing.tsx</code></td><td>Rendered when a TUI plugin navigates to a route id that is not registered. Offers a "go home" action instead of crashing.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="routes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Routes</h2>
    <p>
      The TUI route state lives in <code>context/route.tsx</code>. It is a Solid store initialized
      from <code>NIKCLI_ROUTE</code> when present, otherwise from the home route. Route changes are
      in-memory UI navigation, not browser history.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Route type</th><th>Fields</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>home</code></td><td><code>initialPrompt</code>, <code>workspaceID</code></td><td>Default landing route and empty prompt state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>session</code></td><td><code>sessionID</code>, <code>initialPrompt</code>, <code>workspaceID</code></td><td>Main transcript and prompt view for one session.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>workspace</code></td><td><code>tab</code>, <code>sessionID</code>, <code>workspaceID</code></td><td>Unified workspace panel with tree, changes, graph, and GitHub tabs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tree</code>, <code>changes</code>, <code>git-graph</code>, <code>github</code></td><td><code>sessionID</code>, <code>workspaceID</code></td><td>Legacy route shapes redirected into the workspace view when needed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plugin</code></td><td><code>id</code>, <code>data</code>, <code>workspaceID</code></td><td>TUI plugin routes registered through the plugin runtime.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The app keeps a mutable plugin route map and a reactive route key. When plugin routes change,
      the key is bumped so the route switch re-renders. Missing plugin routes render
      <code>PluginRouteMissing</code> with a home navigation action instead of crashing the TUI.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="commands-keybinds" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Commands and keybinds</h2>
    <p>
      <code>CommandProvider</code> owns the command registry. Components register command options with
      titles, descriptions, optional keybind names, optional slash aliases, categories, visibility,
      and an <code>onSelect</code> callback. The registry deduplicates by category plus value, merges
      local and global registrations, and exposes command palette, slash, and trigger APIs.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Command palette</td><td>The configured <code>command_list</code> keybind opens a <code>DialogSelect</code> of visible commands.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Direct keybinds</td><td>The provider listens to keyboard events, skips disabled commands and active dialogs, and calls the matching command callback.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Slash commands</td><td>Visible command options with <code>slash</code> metadata become prompt slash entries with aliases.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Programmatic trigger</td><td><code>command.trigger(name)</code> runs the matching command; TUI HTTP events use this path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Suspension</td><td><code>keybinds(false)</code> increments a suspend counter so dialogs or text inputs can temporarily suppress command shortcuts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Permission mode keybind</td><td>The <code>permission_mode</code> command opens <code>DialogPermissionMode</code> — see <a href="/docs/permissions#modes">Permission modes</a>. It applies the selected preset (<code>require_approval</code> / <code>approve_for_me</code> / <code>full_access</code>) to the current primary agent.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session dialogs</td><td><code>Context usage</code> (<code>/usage</code> or <code>/context</code>) opens <code>DialogUsage</code>; <code>Analytics</code> (<code>/analytics</code> or <code>/stats</code>) opens <code>DialogAnalytics</code>. See <a href="#dialogs">Dialogs</a> for screenshots.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="dialogs" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Dialogs</h2>
    <p>
      The TUI ships a few session-scoped dialogs that are useful to keep open while running long
      work. They are reachable from the command palette and as slash commands.
    </p>

    <h3>Context usage</h3>
    <p>
      <code>DialogUsage</code> (<code>app.tsx</code> line 1074) shows how the active session is
      spending its 1.0&nbsp;M-token context window. The dialog breaks the window into used / free /
      reserved slices, plots a tokens-per-turn sparkline over the last 77 turns, surfaces the top
      token sources (system prompt, tools, skills, conversation, plus per-tool contributions from
      <code>opentui</code>, <code>terminal-control</code>, <code>generate_image</code>,
      <code>browser</code>, etc.), and ends with a four-card <em>Context health</em> strip
      (Usage, Cache, Headroom, Fragmentation).
    </p>
    <div class="terminal-frame not-prose">
      <a href="/docs/tui/context-usage.png"><img src="/docs/tui/context-usage.png"
      alt="Context usage dialog" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></a>
    </div>

    <h3>Analytics</h3>
    <p>
      <code>DialogAnalytics</code> (<code>app.tsx</code> line 1086) is a 6-tab dashboard loaded
      through <code>AnalyticsProvider</code> against the host's <code>/analytics/*</code> HTTP
      endpoints. The Overview tab shows sessions, messages, cost, tokens with deltas vs. the prior
      window, the last-30-day token and cost histograms, the usage-composition stack
      (input / output / cache / reasoning), and the four-card Operational health strip
      (Activity, Tools, Background, Workspaces). Tabs <code>2&nbsp;Tokens</code>,
      <code>3&nbsp;Models</code>, <code>4&nbsp;Tools</code>, <code>5&nbsp;Projects</code>,
      <code>6&nbsp;Sessions</code> drill into each slice; cycle with <code>tab</code> or
      <code>1</code>&ndash;<code>6</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="terminal-frame not-prose">
      <a href="/docs/tui/analytics-overview.png"><img src="/docs/tui/analytics-overview.png"
      alt="Analytics overview tab" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="event-flow" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Event flow</h2>
    <p>
      <code>SDKProvider</code> creates a generated SDK client and an event emitter keyed by event
      type. It batches event delivery inside Solid <code>batch</code> calls, flushing immediately
      unless another flush happened in the last 16 milliseconds.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Direct mode</strong> passes a custom <code>EventSource</code> from <code>thread.ts</code>; the worker opens the SDK event stream and emits events over RPC.</li>
      <li><strong>HTTP mode</strong> calls <code>sdk.event.subscribe</code> directly and retries with exponential backoff from 250 ms to 5 seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Queueing</strong> batches received events to keep bursty server updates from forcing one render per event.</li>
      <li><strong>TUI commands</strong> listen for <code>tui.command.execute</code>, <code>tui.toast.show</code>, and <code>tui.session.select</code> events and map them to local UI actions.</li>
      <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> aborts the SDK signal, active SSE controller, and pending flush timer on provider unmount.</li>
    </ol>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>TUI event</th><th>Payload</th><th>Local action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>tui.prompt.append</code></td><td><code>text</code></td><td>Append text to the active prompt.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tui.command.execute</code></td><td><code>command</code></td><td>Trigger a registered command such as session list, submit prompt, page up, compact, or agent cycle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tui.toast.show</code></td><td><code>title</code>, <code>message</code>, <code>variant</code>, <code>duration</code></td><td>Render an info, success, warning, or error toast.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tui.session.select</code></td><td><code>sessionID</code></td><td>Navigate to the requested session after the server validates it exists.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Workspace event catch-up">
      <p>
        The session sync layer replays missed events for every workspace when the TUI starts or
        reconnects, so per-project state is identical to the server at render time.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="island" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Island plugin</h2>
    <p>
      The Island plugin is the CLI-side mirror of the macOS <code>NikcliIsland.app</code> notch
      UI. It writes a per-session JSON snapshot under
      <code>~/Library/Application Support/NikcliIsland/state.d/</code> on every relevant event,
      and the native app polls that directory to render live session state without keeping a
      separate IPC channel.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bridge lives in <code>packages/nikcli/src/plugin/island/bridge.ts</code> and is
      self-activating: <code>Bus.publish</code> itself calls <code>start()</code> on first
      publish (idempotent, no-op after the first real call). That matters because the TUI's real
      session / bus activity runs inside a <code>Worker</code> thread with isolated module state —
      a listener registered only in the parent process would never see those events.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Source</th><th>Display</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>idle</code></td><td><code>session.status</code> → <code>idle</code></td><td>Empty pill on the notch.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>thinking</code></td><td><code>session.status</code> → <code>busy</code> / <code>retry</code></td><td>"Thinking…" or "Retrying (N)…".</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tool</code></td><td><code>message.part.updated</code> tool status</td><td>Friendly label plus file basename, lingers for ~1.9 s after completion.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>permission</code></td><td><code>permission.asked</code></td><td>"Awaiting permission" + the request id for one-tap approval.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>error</code></td><td><code>session.error</code></td><td>"Error" pill.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>done</code></td><td>(reserved)</td><td>Used by the native app to mark a completed row before archival.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Each snapshot also carries the parent session id, agent title, project, cwd, the
      process pid, the local server port (when one is running), and a server URL — enough for
      the native app to call <code>POST /permission/:requestID/reply</code> directly when you
      approve a pending request from the notch.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
# Inspect Island state
cat ~/Library/Application\ Support/NikcliIsland/state.d/nikcli-ses_abc.json

# Disable Island for this run without uninstalling the app
NIKCLI_ISLAND_DISABLE=1 nikcli
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="macOS only">
      <p>
        Island is a no-op on Windows and Linux: the native app is a macOS-only notch UI. The
        <code>NIKCLI_ISLAND_TEST_FORCE_DARWIN=1</code> flag exists only for the bridge's own
        test suite — never set it in production.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Toggling from inside the TUI">
      <p>
        The Island feature plugin's activate / deactivate calls
        <code>IslandBridge.setEnabled()</code> — toggling off clears the current snapshots and
        stops writing, toggling back on resumes through the already-registered listener without
        any race window.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="http-control" class="doc-section">
    <h2>HTTP control routes</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/server/routes/tui.ts</code> exposes routes that remote clients,
      companion surfaces, and automation can use to nudge the running TUI. Most routes publish a
      typed <code>TuiEvent</code> onto the bus. <code>/tui/control</code> is a queue bridge for
      request/response handoff.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Publishes or returns</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/append-prompt</code></td><td><code>TuiEvent.PromptAppend</code></td><td>Append text into the active prompt input.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/open-help</code></td><td><code>help.show</code></td><td>Open the help dialog.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/open-sessions</code></td><td><code>session.list</code></td><td>Open session selection.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/open-themes</code></td><td><code>theme.switch</code></td><td>Open theme selection.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/open-models</code></td><td><code>model.list</code></td><td>Open model selection.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/submit-prompt</code></td><td><code>prompt.submit</code></td><td>Submit the current prompt.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/clear-prompt</code></td><td><code>prompt.clear</code></td><td>Clear prompt input.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/execute-command</code></td><td>Mapped command name</td><td>Translate legacy remote names such as <code>agent_cycle</code> into current command ids.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/show-toast</code></td><td><code>TuiEvent.ToastShow</code></td><td>Show a notification in the terminal.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/publish</code></td><td>Any typed <code>TuiEvent</code></td><td>Generic event publish endpoint with zod validation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/select-session</code></td><td><code>TuiEvent.SessionSelect</code></td><td>Validate a session through <code>Session.Service</code>, then navigate the TUI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>GET /tui/control/next</code></td><td>Queued TUI request</td><td>Pull the next queued control request.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /tui/control/response</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td>Push the response for a pending queued request.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="config-plugins" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Config and plugins</h2>
    <p>
      TUI-specific configuration is loaded by <code>packages/nikcli/src/config/tui.ts</code>. The
      loader reads global config directory files, <code>NIKCLI_TUI_CONFIG</code>, project TUI files,
      <code>.nikcli</code> config directories, and managed config. It migrates old TUI config, flattens
      nested <code>tui</code> objects, parses keybinds, deduplicates plugin specs, and records plugin
      metadata with local or global scope.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Config area</th><th>Runtime use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>mouse</code></td><td>Controls OpenTUI mouse support; defaults to enabled.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>keybinds</code></td><td>Parsed through shared config keybind parsing and consumed by <code>KeybindProvider</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scroll_speed</code>, <code>scroll_acceleration</code></td><td>Consumed by session route scroll logic.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>diff_style</code></td><td>Used by session permission and diff rendering components.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plugin</code></td><td>Feeds <code>TuiPluginRuntime.init</code> through <code>createTuiApi</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plugin_meta</code></td><td>Records whether each plugin came from local or global config and the source file.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The TUI plugin API can register commands, plugin routes, and UI contributions. Runtime cleanup
      is tied to exit through <code>TuiPluginRuntime.dispose()</code>, so plugins should not depend on
      process exit for teardown.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remote-attach" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Remote and attach</h2>
    <p>
      The TUI can either host its own local server for external access or attach to an existing
      server. <code>nikcli attach &lt;url&gt;</code> calls the same <code>tui</code> renderer with the
      provided URL instead of starting a local worker server. Remote Control uses TUI commands and
      server routes to drive the active prompt, sessions, scrolling, compacting, sharing, and
      interruption flows.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli --port 4096 --hostname 127.0.0.1
nikcli attach http://127.0.0.1:4096
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4096/tui/open-sessions
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4096/tui/execute-command -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '&#123;"command":"agent_cycle"&#125;'
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Companion is separate">
      <p>
        <code>nikcli companion serve</code> and <code>nikcli companion open</code> start the desktop
        browser companion surface. It connects to the same server/event model, but it is not the
        terminal renderer and it is distinct from the mobile host.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="operational-checks" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Operational checks</h2>
    <p>Use these checks when debugging TUI startup, remote control, or event delivery.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
NIKCLI_TERMINAL=1 bun run dev
nikcli --print-logs --log-level DEBUG
nikcli --port 0 --hostname 127.0.0.1
nikcli attach http://127.0.0.1:4096
curl -i http://127.0.0.1:4096/global
curl -i -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4096/tui/open-help
    </DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>What it proves</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Start without network flags</td><td>Direct worker fetch and worker event subscription work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Start with <code>--port</code></td><td>Worker server startup, HTTP fetch, and server-side SSE work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call <code>/tui/open-help</code></td><td>HTTP route can publish a TUI command event and the renderer is subscribed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call <code>/global</code></td><td>Server auth, request middleware, and basic SDK reachability are healthy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Use <code>SIGUSR2</code></td><td>Worker reload disposes project instances without restarting the renderer process.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>TUI hangs before drawing</td><td>Bun reports non-TTY stdin and no <code>NIKCLI_TERMINAL=1</code>, so the command is waiting for piped stdin.</td><td>Run from an interactive terminal, set <code>NIKCLI_TERMINAL=1</code> for managed PTYs, or provide stdin intentionally.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session id rejected before UI starts</td><td><code>--session</code> failed schema parsing or <code>sdk.session.get</code> returned an error.</td><td>Use a valid <code>ses...</code> id from <code>nikcli session list</code> and confirm the data root/project matches.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Remote button does nothing</td><td>The TUI is in direct mode and no local HTTP server has been started yet, or the server callback failed.</td><td>Start with <code>--port</code> or trigger the on-demand local server path and inspect worker logs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HTTP TUI route returns true but UI does not change</td><td>The server published the event, but the renderer SDK event subscription is disconnected.</td><td>Check SSE reconnect logs, server auth flags, and whether the TUI is attached to the same server URL.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Keyboard shortcut ignored</td><td>A dialog or text input has suspended command keybinds, or the command is disabled for the current route.</td><td>Close the dialog, check route-specific command registration, and inspect TUI keybind config.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plugin route missing</td><td>A plugin navigated to a route id that is not registered in the current plugin route map.</td><td>Verify the TUI plugin was loaded from config and registered its route before navigation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Worker does not exit cleanly</td><td>A server, event stream, or instance finalizer is still shutting down after the five-second timeout.</td><td>Inspect worker logs and confirm server/event streams stop through <code>shutdown</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

